{"duration_api_ms":42513,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"16b83157-11ca-4e2b-be9a-a5b4ac9cf435","total_cost_usd":0.1497036,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22353,"cache_read_input_tokens":38068,"output_tokens":5267,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1200},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22353,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2049,"cache_read_input_tokens":28528,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3365,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3365},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":5267,"cacheReadInputTokens":38068,"cacheCreationInputTokens":22353,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1497036,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1200,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"/tmp mounted on node1 with noexec, nosuid, and nodev enforced\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"fstab entry added (tmpfs /tmp tmpfs defaults,nosuid,nodev,noexec) and live remount confirmed via findmnt showing rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec on /tmp. Functional test placing an executable script in /tmp failed with Permission denied (exit 126), directly demonstrating noexec enforcement. nosuid/nodev were present as mount flags in findmnt output; mknod succeeded (expected, since nodev blocks device-node *usage* not creation) but the flag itself is directly observed in findmnt, which is the primary evidence for this requirement.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-291d184904cc4602\",\"cmd-75567d5d8ca74368\",\"cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"/dev/shm mounted on node1 with noexec, nosuid, and nodev enforced\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"fstab entry added for /dev/shm with defaults,nosuid,nodev,noexec, and live remount confirmed via findmnt showing rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec on /dev/shm. Functional test executing a script placed in /dev/shm failed with Permission denied (exit 126), confirming noexec enforcement in practice.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-291d184904cc4602\",\"cmd-75567d5d8ca74368\",\"cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Hardened mount settings for /tmp and /dev/shm survive a reboot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A healthy pre-restart baseline was captured (boot_id 98aa35f5..., mount options confirmed healthy). systemctl reboot was issued and acknowledged, the node became unreachable during the reboot window, then reconnected with a new boot_id (662e94b4...), proving a genuine restart occurred. Post-reboot findmnt confirmed both /tmp and /dev/shm retained rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec, and the functional execution-denial tests were re-run and again failed with Permission denied, directly demonstrating that the hardening persisted across the full disruption/restoration cycle. This transition also satisfies the independent node-restart evidence protocol for the sole managed node, node1, cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare (98aa35f5...) and after-executor (662e94b4...) boot-identity snapshots, which match the executor's observed values.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9641787808664d81\",\"cmd-5e520cfa45974122\",\"cmd-d3a3e7eb14834630\",\"cmd-d9bfa61dc02745ba\",\"cmd-be8c256794b74cc0\",\"cmd-1ea766be3d1d44c4\",\"cmd-a4a7ed60c1e74d02\",\"cmd-726b83ffc1994db9\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All executor commands trace clearly to task fulfillment: baseline discovery, an fstab backup before editing (a safe, reversible precaution, not residue), the fstab edit itself, live remount, functional verification, the reboot required to prove persistence, and post-reboot re-verification. Test scripts and device nodes created for functional testing (cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9) were cleaned up immediately (cmd-08d8b645c34c4128, and inline rm in cmd-726b83ffc1994db9). The only residue is the timestamped /etc/fstab.bak.* backup file from cmd-291d184904cc4602, which is a standard, low-risk, intentional safety artifact rather than unwanted mutation. The lifecycle diff shows no unexpected changes beyond boot_id, ephemeral systemd-private tmp directories, and PID/port churn from the reboot itself, all expected. No unnecessary, broad, or destructive mutations were found.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-291d184904cc4602\",\"cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9\",\"cmd-08d8b645c34c4128\",\"cmd-726b83ffc1994db9\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor hardened /tmp and /dev/shm on node1 with noexec,nosuid,nodev via /etc/fstab, applied the changes live, and proved enforcement functionally (script execution denied) both before and after an actual reboot with a verified boot_id change. All three material outcomes of the task — noexec/nosuid/nodev on /tmp, the same on /dev/shm, and persistence across reboot — are directly and coherently demonstrated. Operational hygiene is clean, with only an intentional fstab backup left behind and all test artifacts removed.\",\"limitations\":[\"The nodev enforcement was validated only via the mount flag shown in findmnt; the functional test only confirmed mknod could create a device-node file (expected under nodev, since nodev blocks device semantics on read/write, not node creation) and did not attempt to open/use the device node to prove nodev's runtime effect.\",\"Only one managed node (node1) exists in this topology, so the reboot performed to validate persistence doubles as the provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol; no additional nodes required separate restart evidence.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"/tmp mounted on node1 with noexec, nosuid, and nodev enforced","status":"satisfied","summary":"fstab entry added (tmpfs /tmp tmpfs defaults,nosuid,nodev,noexec) and live remount confirmed via findmnt showing rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec on /tmp. Functional test placing an executable script in /tmp failed with Permission denied (exit 126), directly demonstrating noexec enforcement. nosuid/nodev were present as mount flags in findmnt output; mknod succeeded (expected, since nodev blocks device-node *usage* not creation) but the flag itself is directly observed in findmnt, which is the primary evidence for this requirement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-291d184904cc4602","cmd-75567d5d8ca74368","cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"/dev/shm mounted on node1 with noexec, nosuid, and nodev enforced","status":"satisfied","summary":"fstab entry added for /dev/shm with defaults,nosuid,nodev,noexec, and live remount confirmed via findmnt showing rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec on /dev/shm. Functional test executing a script placed in /dev/shm failed with Permission denied (exit 126), confirming noexec enforcement in practice.","evidence_ids":["cmd-291d184904cc4602","cmd-75567d5d8ca74368","cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Hardened mount settings for /tmp and /dev/shm survive a reboot","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy pre-restart baseline was captured (boot_id 98aa35f5..., mount options confirmed healthy). systemctl reboot was issued and acknowledged, the node became unreachable during the reboot window, then reconnected with a new boot_id (662e94b4...), proving a genuine restart occurred. Post-reboot findmnt confirmed both /tmp and /dev/shm retained rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec, and the functional execution-denial tests were re-run and again failed with Permission denied, directly demonstrating that the hardening persisted across the full disruption/restoration cycle. This transition also satisfies the independent node-restart evidence protocol for the sole managed node, node1, cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare (98aa35f5...) and after-executor (662e94b4...) boot-identity snapshots, which match the executor's observed values.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9641787808664d81","cmd-5e520cfa45974122","cmd-d3a3e7eb14834630","cmd-d9bfa61dc02745ba","cmd-be8c256794b74cc0","cmd-1ea766be3d1d44c4","cmd-a4a7ed60c1e74d02","cmd-726b83ffc1994db9","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All executor commands trace clearly to task fulfillment: baseline discovery, an fstab backup before editing (a safe, reversible precaution, not residue), the fstab edit itself, live remount, functional verification, the reboot required to prove persistence, and post-reboot re-verification. Test scripts and device nodes created for functional testing (cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9) were cleaned up immediately (cmd-08d8b645c34c4128, and inline rm in cmd-726b83ffc1994db9). The only residue is the timestamped /etc/fstab.bak.* backup file from cmd-291d184904cc4602, which is a standard, low-risk, intentional safety artifact rather than unwanted mutation. The lifecycle diff shows no unexpected changes beyond boot_id, ephemeral systemd-private tmp directories, and PID/port churn from the reboot itself, all expected. No unnecessary, broad, or destructive mutations were found.","evidence_ids":["cmd-291d184904cc4602","cmd-a4ab5554c8bd49f9","cmd-08d8b645c34c4128","cmd-726b83ffc1994db9"]},"overall_summary":"The executor hardened /tmp and /dev/shm on node1 with noexec,nosuid,nodev via /etc/fstab, applied the changes live, and proved enforcement functionally (script execution denied) both before and after an actual reboot with a verified boot_id change. All three material outcomes of the task — noexec/nosuid/nodev on /tmp, the same on /dev/shm, and persistence across reboot — are directly and coherently demonstrated. Operational hygiene is clean, with only an intentional fstab backup left behind and all test artifacts removed.","limitations":["The nodev enforcement was validated only via the mount flag shown in findmnt; the functional test only confirmed mknod could create a device-node file (expected under nodev, since nodev blocks device semantics on read/write, not node creation) and did not attempt to open/use the device node to prove nodev's runtime effect.","Only one managed node (node1) exists in this topology, so the reboot performed to validate persistence doubles as the provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol; no additional nodes required separate restart evidence."]},"ttft_ms":12184,"type":"result","duration_ms":42712,"uuid":"ce6fb5e9-97e4-4892-b98c-e52e84941707","ttft_stream_ms":755,"time_to_request_ms":187,"first_content_frame_ms":755,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
